  "narrative": "During the 1970s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture flourished as a celebration of their natural beauty and identity. The afro, a hairstyle created by combing out natural growth of afro-textured hair, was a prominent symbol of black pride and self-empowerment. Cornrows, a traditional style of three-strand braids originating from Africa, were also popularized during this era as a way to showcase African cultural heritage. The Jheri curl, invented by Jheri Redding in the 1970s (though its popularity peaked in the 1980s), was another hairstyle that gained traction among Black Americans. While documentation on hairstyles and hair culture from the 1970s is limited within this source material, it is clear that this decade marked a significant shift towards embracing natural textures and African diasporic traditions. As women of color sought to reclaim their identities and resist Eurocentric beauty standards, hairstyles like the afro, cornrows, and Jheri curl became powerful expressions of resistance and self-love.",
